          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The short version: Tekton and Icon make hand tools of genuinely similar quality, and the steel is not what should decide this for you. What should decide it is how you buy. Tekton is the better choice if you build a kit deliberately, online, and want the easiest warranty in the business. Icon is the better choice if there is a Harbor Freight ten minutes away and you value walking out with a tool, or a replacement, the same afternoon.
        </p>

        <div id="who-they-are" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who Each Brand Actually Is
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tekton</h3>

          <p>
            Tekton is a family-owned company based in Grand Rapids, Michigan, and it sells almost entirely online: through its own website, Amazon, and the web storefronts of other retailers. There is no Tekton store and no tool truck, which is the whole commercial point. Removing the dealer and the franchisee from the chain is how Tekton puts good chrome vanadium in your hand for well under what a truck brand charges. Our full <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">Tekton tools review</a> covers the range and its history in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other thing that defines Tekton is how it organizes its catalog. Instead of selling a 200-piece mechanic's kit with a ratchet, three extensions and forty sockets you will never use, it sells by tool and by format: sockets on rails or in trays, wrenches in holders, ratchets on their own. You buy what you need, in the drive sizes you need, and add to it later. That suits a deliberate buyer and frustrates someone who wants one box that does everything on day one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Icon</h3>

          <p>
            Icon is Harbor Freight's own premium hand-tool brand. It sits above Pittsburgh and Pittsburgh Pro in the store's hierarchy and is pitched squarely at people who would otherwise buy from a tool truck. You can only buy it from Harbor Freight, either in one of its stores or on its website. That exclusivity cuts both ways: you cannot shop it around for a better price, but you can walk into a store on a Saturday afternoon and leave with the tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Icon's reputation was built on its ratchets and its roller cabinets, and Harbor Freight has kept investing in the ratchets in particular. The second-generation G2 line launched in spring 2025 and has been expanding since, with Harbor Freight announcing more G2 models through 2026. Sockets, combination and ratcheting wrenches, torque wrenches, pliers and screwdrivers fill out the rest of the line, and the range on any given store shelf depends on that store.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the comparison is not really one American brand against one import brand, or cheap against premium. Both are mid-to-upper tier hand tools made largely in Taiwan to a specification set by a US company. The real differences are in specific mechanisms, in how you pay, and in what happens when something breaks. That is what the rest of this page is about.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Head-to-Head Comparison
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tekton</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Icon</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Who sells it</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tekton direct, Amazon, retailer websites</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Harbor Freight stores and website only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3/8 in. ratchet mechanism</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90-tooth, 4-degree arc (current quick-release line)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies by model: 72-tooth/5-degree to 90-tooth/4-degree</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Socket design</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-point, chrome plated, large stamped markings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-point, chrome moly, high polish, thin wall</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Combination wrenches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-point box offset 15 degrees, 15-degree open end</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-point box, 15-degree offset, angled open end</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pricing style</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stable list price, periodic online sales</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">List price plus frequent coupons and member pricing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Warranty</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime, no receipt, ships replacements free</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime for original purchaser, exchanged in store</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Main country of origin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mostly Taiwan, some USA and others</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Taiwan</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read the table as a list of trade-offs rather than a scorecard. On the core hand-tool specifications the two brands land close to each other: both use 6-point sockets, both use 15-degree box offsets, both quote 4-degree arc swings on their best ratchets. Where they diverge is the mechanism range at the edges of the catalog, the finish, and the buying experience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices are deliberately left out of the table because they move, and they move differently for each brand. Tekton tends to hold a list price and run sales online. Icon's shelf price is often not the price anyone pays, because Harbor Freight runs coupons and member deals on Icon tools frequently. Comparing sticker prices on a random Tuesday will mislead you in either direction.
          </p>

          <p>
            The specifications quoted here come from each brand's own product listings and announcements. Neither brand publishes torque-to-failure figures in a form that allows a clean comparison, so we do not invent one. Where owners consistently report a strength or weakness, we say it is owner-reported rather than dressing it up as a measurement.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy the mechanism, not the logo.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Within this price tier, the specific tool matters more than the brand. A 72-tooth Icon stubby and a 90-tooth Tekton full-size ratchet are different tools for different jobs, whatever the badge says.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ratchets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Ratchets: Tooth Count, Swing Arc and Feel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tooth count decides how far you have to swing the handle before the pawl catches the next tooth. Divide 360 by the number of teeth and you have the arc: a 90-tooth gear needs 4 degrees, a 72-tooth gear needs 5. In an open engine bay that difference is invisible. Behind a water pump or up against a firewall, where you might have a couple of inches of handle travel, it decides whether the fastener turns at all. Our guide to <a href="/ratchet-tooth-count-explained/">ratchet tooth count</a> walks through the trade-off between fine teeth and tooth strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tekton's current quick-release ratchets use a 90-tooth gear with a 4-degree swing arc and a single half-moon pawl that keeps nine teeth engaged with the gear at once. More engaged teeth spread the load, which is how a fine-tooth design stays strong. Some older Tekton ratchets and sets used 72-tooth mechanisms with a 5-degree arc, so check the listing on the exact ratchet you are buying. Tekton also sells service kits for its ratchets, which is a quietly useful detail if you would rather rebuild than replace.
          </p>

          <p>
            Icon's ratchet range is broader and less uniform. The first-generation Icon Professional ratchets used a 90-tooth gear with a 4-degree arc. The G2 line that replaced them uses split-pawl technology, a nickel-chrome-moly gear and a fully sealed head, but tooth count varies by model: Harbor Freight lists 72 teeth and a 5-degree arc on the 1/4 in. drive G2 ratchets and 90 teeth with a 4-degree arc on the 26 in. 3/4 in. drive model. If swing arc matters for the job you have in mind, read the spec line on the individual tool rather than assuming every Icon ratchet is the same.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Icon clearly leads is variety. The G2 lineup includes stubbies, standard handles, comfort grips, long and extra-long reach, and locking flex heads with nine lock positions across a 132-degree range that swing a full 180 degrees when unlocked. Tekton makes standard, comfort-grip, flex-head and stubby folding ratchets, but it does not field as many specialty shapes. If you want a long locking-flex 3/8 in. ratchet specifically, Icon has more to choose from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feel is subjective, and the honest answer is that owners of both brands report smooth action and low back drag on current models; Harbor Freight specifically says the G2 was engineered to reduce back drag so a loose fastener spins off rather than turning with the ratchet. The ratchets we have compared here top out at 90 teeth; 120-position dual-pawl designs from other brands swing in about 3 degrees, so if you work in very tight spaces every day, that class of tool is a different conversation. For most home and independent mechanics, either brand's 90-tooth ratchet is enough.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sockets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sockets: Finish, Markings and Fit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands lead with 6-point sockets for hand use, which is the right call. A 6-point grips the flats of a hex fastener rather than its corners, so it is far less likely to round off a stuck bolt. If you are still deciding between geometries, our guide to <a href="/6-point-vs-12-point-sockets/">6-point vs 12-point sockets</a> explains when a 12-point is still worth owning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tekton's chrome sockets use a 6-point opening with radiused corners, so force goes onto the flat sides of the fastener. The markings are large and roll-stamped into the metal, which means they cannot wear off the way printed or lightly etched markings can, and Tekton sizes them to stay readable around the curve of the socket. Four detent grooves on the drive end give the ratchet's retaining ball a secure catch, and the inner rim is beveled to guide the socket onto the drive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Icon's hand sockets are chrome moly with a high-polish finish, a 6-point design, chamfered openings and thin walls. Thin walls are the practical advantage: they reach fasteners recessed into a casting or tucked against a bracket where a thicker socket will not fit. The high polish wipes clean easily, and owners like how the size markings read. The material is a small talking point; chrome moly is tougher and less brittle, though for hand sockets that are not going on an impact gun both alloys do the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger difference is how the sockets are sold. Tekton sells sockets on rails, in trays and as individual pieces, organized by drive size and depth, so filling a gap in a set is easy and the format suits a drawer system. Icon sells sockets in sets, often in trays or modular organizers, and those sets go on promotion regularly. If you are buying a complete 3/8 in. set in one go, Icon on a coupon can be very good value. If you are building out a specific range, Tekton's by-the-piece approach wastes less money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever brand you pick, choose the drive size before the brand. Most home mechanics are best served by a full 3/8 in. set, a 1/4 in. set for small fasteners and a 1/2 in. set for suspension and lug nuts. Our guide to <a href="/what-drive-size-socket-set-do-i-need/">which drive size socket set you need</a> sets out what goes in each, and it applies equally to both brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrenches" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Wrenches, Torque Wrenches and the Rest
          </h2>

          <p>
            On paper, the combination wrenches are almost interchangeable. Tekton's are chrome vanadium with a mirror chrome finish, a 12-point box end offset 15 degrees for knuckle clearance, and an open end angled 15 degrees so you can flip the wrench to catch the next flat in a tight space. Icon's Professional combination wrenches are chrome vanadium with a polished chrome finish, a 12-point box end, a 15-degree offset and an angled open end. If anyone tells you one of these brands is dramatically better at plain combination wrenches, ask them what they measured.
          </p>

          <p>
            Icon does offer more variants, including anti-slip grip sets with a modified open-end jaw and ratcheting combination wrench sets. Tekton makes ratcheting wrenches too, but if you specifically want a gripping open-end profile, Icon is the brand of the two that sells one. Tekton's wrench sets come in a holder with a folding handle and non-slip grippers, which is a small thing that makes a big difference when the wrenches live in a drawer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-tekton-hand-tools/">best Tekton hand tools</a> guide picks the Tekton combination wrench set as the best overall Tekton tool for exactly this reason: it is the category where the gap to premium brands is smallest and the saving is largest. That is true of Icon's sets as well, so on wrenches the decision really does come down to price on the day and where you prefer to shop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands also make click torque wrenches, and here we would be more cautious about choosing by brand. A torque wrench is a measuring instrument; what matters is the stated accuracy, the range, whether it ships with a calibration certificate and whether you can get it recalibrated. Check those details on the specific model you are looking at, and see our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">best torque wrench</a> guide for what to look for.
          </p>

          <p>
            For screwdrivers, pliers and hex keys, the same logic holds. Neither brand is a specialist in these categories the way Knipex is in pliers or Wera is in drivers, and both make sound everyday versions. If you already own a mostly Tekton kit, keep it consistent; if you live near a Harbor Freight and already own Icon, there is no reason to switch for the sake of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Price, Buying and Warranty
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prices for both brands move, so treat any figure here as a range rather than a quote. As of this writing, a Tekton 3/8 in. drive 8 in. quick-release ratchet lists at around $40 to $45 at major online retailers, and a 15-piece Tekton combination wrench set sits in the mid-$90s. On the Icon side, Harbor Freight has listed G2 ratchets from about $30 for a 1/4 in. stubby to around $120 for the 26 in. 3/4 in. drive model, and a 14-piece Icon Professional combination wrench set around $100 before any discount.
          </p>

          <p>
            The structural difference is coupons. Harbor Freight runs promotions on Icon constantly, and its Inside Track Club membership adds member-only pricing on top. Patient Icon buyers rarely pay shelf price. Tekton's prices are steadier, with sales on its own site and on Amazon around the usual shopping holidays. If you are willing to wait for a deal, Icon's effective price can drop below Tekton's on a like-for-like tool; if you buy when you need something, Tekton's list price is usually the lower one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Availability is where Icon has a real advantage. Harbor Freight has stores across the country, and if you round off a socket on a Sunday morning you can have a replacement in your hand before lunch. Tekton ships quickly, but it still ships. For a professional who loses money every hour a tool is missing, that difference can matter more than anything on the spec sheet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands back their hand tools with a lifetime warranty, but the process is different. Tekton's warranty has no time limit and needs no receipt: you send a photo of the broken tool with your name and the date in the shot, fill in an online form, and Tekton ships the replacement at its own cost, the same day if the claim is complete and arrives before 2 p.m. Eastern on a weekday. Harbor Freight warrants Icon hand tools for the lifetime of the original purchaser and handles claims at the store counter; its written policy asks for the receipt and reserves the right to substitute a similar item if the exact one is not available, though owners widely report receipt-free exchanges in practice.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What Each Warranty Asks For</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Photo + form</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tekton claim, no receipt</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Same day</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tekton ship time on complete weekday claims</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">In store</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Icon exchange at a Harbor Freight counter</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Neither warranty is better in every situation. Tekton's is the least bureaucratic in the industry and works the same wherever you live, but you wait for the parcel. Icon's gets you a tool the same day if your local store has it in stock, but it depends on that store's stock and staff. Pick the one whose failure mode bothers you less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where They Are Made and Who Should Buy Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands are largely made in Taiwan, which has been producing high-grade hand tools for decades and supplies many brands people consider premium. Icon tools are marked made in Taiwan and are believed to come from several established Taiwanese factories, while Harbor Freight describes the G2 ratchets as designed and engineered in the USA. Tekton sources most of its range from Taiwan, makes a smaller and growing share in the United States, and lists the country of origin on every product page. Our guide to <a href="/where-are-tekton-tools-made/">where Tekton tools are made</a> breaks that down by category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Country of origin should not be the tie-breaker here. Two Taiwanese factories working to two different specifications can produce very different tools, and the same factory can produce a good tool and a poor one depending on what the brand asks for. Judge each brand by its specifications, its warranty and owner experience, which is what this page has tried to do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Tekton if</h3>

          <p>
            You are building a kit deliberately and want to buy exactly the sockets, wrenches and ratchets you need, in organized formats that drop straight into a drawer. You do not live near a Harbor Freight, or you would rather not wait for coupons. You want the simplest warranty claim available and do not mind waiting a couple of days for the replacement. And you want consistent mechanisms: a current Tekton 3/8 in. ratchet is a 90-tooth, 4-degree tool without having to read the fine print model by model. Start with our <a href="/best-tekton-hand-tools/">best Tekton hand tools</a> picks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Icon if</h3>

          <p>
            There is a Harbor Freight nearby and you value same-day purchases and same-day warranty swaps. You want a specialty ratchet shape, such as a long locking-flex head or an extra-long reach, that Tekton does not make. You are buying a complete set in one go and are happy to wait for a coupon to get it at a strong price. Or you already own some Icon and want your kit to match.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are torn, there is no penalty for mixing brands: a 3/8 in. socket fits a 3/8 in. ratchet regardless of the logo. Plenty of good toolboxes hold a Tekton wrench set and an Icon flex-head ratchet side by side. If you are weighing Tekton against other mid-tier and premium brands as well, our comparisons of <a href="/tekton-vs-gearwrench/">Tekton vs GearWrench</a> and <a href="/tekton-vs-snap-on/">Tekton vs Snap-on</a> cover the same ground from different angles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Icon ratchet has the same mechanism</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Icon's tooth count varies by model, from 72 teeth and a 5-degree arc on the 1/4 in. G2 ratchets to 90 teeth and 4 degrees on the larger ones. If you are buying for tight engine-bay work, read the spec line on the exact ratchet before you pay rather than assuming the badge guarantees the swing arc.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing shelf price to shelf price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Icon's sticker price is rarely what careful buyers pay, because Harbor Freight runs coupons and member pricing on it regularly. Tekton's list price is steadier. Compare the price you would actually pay this week for the same tool, not two numbers pulled from different days and different promotions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a giant mixed kit you will half use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Huge all-in-one sets look like value until you count the sockets you never touch and the duplicates you already own. Both brands sell focused sets by drive size. Decide on the drive sizes and depths you need first, then buy those, and you will usually spend less for a better kit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating country of origin as a quality grade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both brands are mostly made in Taiwan, as are many tools sold at much higher prices. The specification the brand sets, its quality control and its warranty tell you far more about a tool than the stamp on the handle. Judge the tool in front of you, not the country it came from.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring how you will claim the warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lifetime warranty is only as good as the process behind it. Tekton's needs a photo and a form and arrives by post; Icon's needs a trip to the store and depends on stock. If you have no Harbor Freight nearby, or you need replacements the same day, that should weigh heavily in your choice.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Icon better than Tekton?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better across the board. The core specifications are close: both use 6-point sockets, 15-degree box offsets and 4-degree arcs on their 90-tooth ratchets. Icon offers more ratchet variety and same-day in-store availability. Tekton offers steadier pricing, consistent mechanisms across its ratchet line and the easiest warranty claim. Choose based on how you buy and what you need.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Icon ratchets 90-tooth?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some are, not all. The original Icon Professional ratchets used a 90-tooth gear with a 4-degree arc. In the current G2 line, Harbor Freight lists 72 teeth and a 5-degree arc on the 1/4 in. drive models and 90 teeth with a 4-degree arc on the large 3/4 in. drive model. Check the individual product listing for the ratchet you want.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many teeth does a Tekton ratchet have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tekton's current quick-release ratchets use a 90-tooth gear with a 4-degree swing arc and a single half-moon pawl that keeps nine teeth engaged at once. Some older Tekton ratchets and sets used 72-tooth mechanisms with a 5-degree arc, so confirm the tooth count on the specific listing, especially when buying a ratchet bundled in a set.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you buy Tekton at Harbor Freight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Harbor Freight sells its own house brands, with Icon at the top of its hand-tool range. Tekton sells through its own website, Amazon and the online storefronts of other retailers. Icon, in turn, is sold only through Harbor Freight's stores and website, so you will not find it on Amazon or at another tool retailer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a receipt for an Icon or Tekton warranty claim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tekton says no receipt is needed; you submit a photo of the broken tool with your name and the date, and it ships a replacement for free. Harbor Freight's written warranty asks for the receipt and covers the original purchaser, though many owners report swapping broken Icon hand tools at the counter without one. Keep receipts for Icon to be safe.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Icon and Tekton made in the same factories?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are largely made in Taiwan, and Icon is believed to come from several Taiwanese manufacturers, but neither brand publishes a full list of its factories, so we cannot say whether any overlap. Tekton also makes a smaller share of its tools in the United States and lists the country of origin on each product page.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for a beginner, Tekton or Icon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most beginners, whichever one you can buy most conveniently. If you have a Harbor Freight nearby, an Icon 3/8 in. socket set and ratchet bought on a coupon is a strong start. If not, a Tekton 3/8 in. socket set on rails plus a 90-tooth ratchet gives you a tidy, consistent kit with a warranty you can claim from anywhere.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

